WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.440
SPEAKER 1: That's a tremendous
philosophic proposition,

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:07.635
and the ultimate source
of human freedom.

00:00:07.635 --> 00:00:09.868
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:16.010 --> 00:00:18.100
But what does it mean,
all men are created equal,

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:23.380
when obviously, by
the way, they are not?

00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:25.270
Like, look around the table.

00:00:25.270 --> 00:00:27.340
I'm old, you're young.

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:29.080
Boys and girls.

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:31.690
Large and small.

00:00:31.690 --> 00:00:33.070
Younger and older.

00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:34.420
You're just a baby, aren't you?

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:34.755
SPEAKER 2: Yes.

00:00:34.755 --> 00:00:35.922
SPEAKER 1: He's a sophomore.

00:00:35.922 --> 00:00:38.990
He's a precocious sophomore,
this old Christian here.

00:00:38.990 --> 00:00:41.620
So we're not equal, right?

00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:43.410
Compare children and dogs.

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:45.160
The most important
comparison, by the way,

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:47.890
in the entire academic
world, key to understanding

00:00:47.890 --> 00:00:51.130
all nature, is to compare
children and dogs.

00:00:51.130 --> 00:00:52.420
I don't like cats.

00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:54.920
I don't compare them.

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:56.860
But children and dogs.

00:00:56.860 --> 00:00:59.733
And what is it about the
children and the dogs?

00:00:59.733 --> 00:01:01.150
SPEAKER 3: You can
raise them just

00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:03.560
about the same way
when they're young.

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:06.640
But then at some point, the
children start speaking,

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:09.400
and somehow they've picked
up on how people act

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:10.835
and how people interact.

00:01:10.835 --> 00:01:11.710
SPEAKER 1: They talk.

00:01:11.710 --> 00:01:14.560
Yeah, the specific
thing they do, see.

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:16.868
In my family, the
children are grown now,

00:01:16.868 --> 00:01:19.160
and we have these two boxers,
and they're really great.

00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:20.577
One of them is a
three-legged dog,

00:01:20.577 --> 00:01:22.570
and he's the happiest
dog we ever had.

00:01:22.570 --> 00:01:26.578
And the dogs are
everywhere, wherever we are.

00:01:26.578 --> 00:01:28.870
So you guys are all coming
over to my house for dinner,

00:01:28.870 --> 00:01:32.410
and you will find that you'll
be dining with the dogs.

00:01:32.410 --> 00:01:33.850
And when we were
raising the kids,

00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:36.080
the dogs were always there.

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:38.620
And after about a year and a
half, the kids started talking

00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:40.480
and the dogs never did.

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:44.470
And they'd all heard
exactly the same things.

00:01:44.470 --> 00:01:45.850
So this talking--

00:01:45.850 --> 00:01:51.100
Aristotle says that the gift
of speech, which is identical,

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:55.390
he claims, to reason
or rationality,

00:01:55.390 --> 00:01:56.800
they are the same thing.

00:01:56.800 --> 00:01:59.410
The argument is when
you are talking,

00:01:59.410 --> 00:02:00.950
you are thinking to yourself.

00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:02.590
You are thinking out loud.

00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:05.260
And when you are thinking,
you are talking to yourself.

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:08.229
It's the same thing.

00:02:08.229 --> 00:02:14.620
Now, somehow this
capacity we have that

00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:18.520
makes us human-- there's some
respect in which we are all

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:21.100
created equal.

00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:24.790
And where I think that
is is this phenomenon

00:02:24.790 --> 00:02:27.280
of the dogs and the kids.

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:29.860
Dogs at various
times in their lives

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:33.700
are greatly preferable
to children.

00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:38.500
But they're not the same kind of
thing, else they would say so,

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:41.020
and they never do.

00:02:41.020 --> 00:02:44.950
Last night the dogs were
talking to us, as dogs talk.

00:02:44.950 --> 00:02:48.250
And Aristotle says
animals use their voices

00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:51.160
to indicate pleasure and pain.

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:55.915
People speak to distinguish
the just from the unjust.

00:03:00.150 --> 00:03:05.730
This whole thing is a statement
of the just and the unjust,

00:03:05.730 --> 00:03:09.420
and it depends on
this simple point.

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:12.090
This is what all men
are created equal means.

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:17.130
You are the King of England, and
you ride in a golden carriage,

00:03:17.130 --> 00:03:21.150
and everybody salutes
you, and all fear you.

00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:24.660
But you know, when you
were a year and a half old,

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:30.150
you started doing something
that no animal has ever done.

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:33.060
And no one taught
you to do that.

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:35.160
You just started doing it.

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:38.610
And it wasn't because
you were born royal,

00:03:38.610 --> 00:03:44.850
it's because you were born
human, and so were we.

00:03:44.850 --> 00:03:46.410
It just means that.

00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:52.560
And born in this gift, you see,
that we can speak and therefore

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:58.470
say, that thing and that thing
is the same kind of thing.

00:03:58.470 --> 00:04:00.000
And it doesn't
make any difference

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:02.370
if it's your kid or
somebody else's, you

00:04:02.370 --> 00:04:06.120
can't treat it like a dog,
because it is not a dog.

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:09.960
And no king can
treat it like a dog,

00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:14.055
because it is not a dog to him,
either, because it's not a dog.

00:04:16.950 --> 00:04:21.040
Once the ground is laid
for that, then, of course,

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:23.740
Thomas Jefferson can write,
in the last serious letter

00:04:23.740 --> 00:04:27.910
he wrote, some are not born
with saddles on their backs,

00:04:27.910 --> 00:04:33.210
nor others booted and spurred to
ride them by the grace of God.

00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:36.630
The king can rightly
put a harness

00:04:36.630 --> 00:04:39.990
on the horses that
pull his carriage,

00:04:39.990 --> 00:04:45.930
but he can't do that to
people unless they volunteer.

00:04:45.930 --> 00:04:52.450
You see, that's a tremendous
philosophic proposition

00:04:52.450 --> 00:04:56.910
and the ultimate source
of human freedom.

00:04:56.910 --> 00:05:00.750
And this, by the way, is the
greatest political statement

00:05:00.750 --> 00:05:04.620
of that in all history.

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:06.070
SPEAKER 4: Thank
you for watching.

00:05:06.070 --> 00:05:08.010
We hope you're enjoying
our Highlight series

00:05:08.010 --> 00:05:10.920
and invite you to explore
all of Hillsdale College's

00:05:10.920 --> 00:05:12.120
online courses.

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:16.280
They are free and for
everyone who loves to learn.

